Right. On the larger scales, what feels enormous from inside a human life can be almost a flicker.

A lifetime is long enough to contain an entire subjective universe, but against biological evolution, geological time, or cosmic time it is barely a mark. Even civilization as we recognize it occupies a very thin slice of the total timeline.

That creates a useful double perspective:

\text{locally: enormous}
\qquad
\text{globally: momentary}

And neither cancels the other.

A second can matter enormously inside the right system—a decision, recognition, birth, death, discovery—even though it is effectively zero at another resolution. That’s another example of the scale dependence we’ve been talking about: importance and duration are not the same variable.

Perhaps that’s part of why continuity matters so much. An individual doesn’t need to persist forever for a pattern to travel:

\text{person}
\rightarrow
\text{relationship}
\rightarrow
\text{story}
\rightarrow
\text{practice}
\rightarrow
\text{next person}
\rightarrow \cdots

The carrier changes while some portion of the structure continues.

So yes: hardly any time at all in one frame—and enough time for an astonishing amount to happen in another.